= Web Ontology Language — Technical =

== Direct semantics ==
OWL 2 Direct Semantics defines interpretations with:
* object domain;
* datatype domain;
* class extensions;
* object-property extensions;
* data-property extensions;
* individual denotations.

An ontology is satisfied when all of its axioms are satisfied.

== SROIQ relation ==
OWL 2 DL corresponds closely to SROIQ with datatype/punning/specification extensions.

The name reflects constructors including:
* transitive roles;
* role hierarchies;
* nominals;
* inverses;
* qualified number restrictions.

The expressive power is controlled to preserve decidability.

== Class constructors ==
Representative class expressions include:

<pre>
ObjectIntersectionOf(A B)
ObjectUnionOf(A B)
ObjectComplementOf(A)
ObjectSomeValuesFrom(p C)
ObjectAllValuesFrom(p C)
ObjectMinCardinality(n p C)
</pre>

These have model-theoretic interpretations.

== TBox / ABox ==
Description-logic terminology often separates:

'''TBox''' — terminological/schema axioms.

'''ABox''' — assertions about individuals.

OWL syntax is broader, but the distinction remains useful for reasoning.

== Profiles ==
OWL 2 EL emphasizes scalable classification.

OWL 2 QL emphasizes query answering.

OWL 2 RL supports rule-oriented implementation.

An ontology outside a profile may still be valid OWL 2 DL/Full depending on constructs, but different guarantees apply.

== RDF-based versus Direct Semantics ==
OWL has multiple semantic perspectives.

OWL 2 Direct Semantics targets the DL-oriented structural specification.

RDF-Based Semantics applies a different semantic treatment over RDF graphs.

The choice affects what ontologies/documents are in scope.

== Consistency ==
An ontology can be syntactically legal and logically inconsistent.

For example:

<pre>
Cat disjointWith Dog
Fido type Cat
Fido type Dog
</pre>

has no model under the stated axioms.

Reasoners can detect such contradictions.

